## Limits

Limits describe the behaviour of fuctions as they approach a value. Not all functions may be defined at certain positions thus 
limts are used to tell what the value would be expected based on the general trends around the point. Limits look like

$$
\lim_{x \to 0} f(x)
$$

Where Lim represents the limmit of a function (f(x)) where x approachs 0. When finding limits the goal is to get infinetly close
to the value needed. A better approximation is always there. For example finding the limit for x approaching 3 we can use 2.9 for 
an approximation. Then again 2.99 would be better 2.999 would be beteer and so on. We cant use 3 as it may not exist. Same can be
done from the other side : 3.1, 3.01, 3.001, ... . Do note that limits for discountinous functions (functions that may not be continous
or follow a trend) can have limits. For example the function x squared but at x=1 y=5 instead of 1. These functions do exist and at x=1 
instead of y being 1 sqaured which is 1 there will be a gap and the point will be at 5. A limit for this function doess exist which is 1.
However a limit of a function does not exxist if both sides approach different values. For example for a random function with limit
of x approaching 2 the limit from 2.1, 2.01, 2.001 may approach to 9 but from below 1.9, 1.99, 1.999 x may approch 6. This function
will not have a limit. And yes this function does exist. It could look like

$$
f(x) = \begin{cases} x^2 + 2 & x < 2 \\ 2x^2 + 1 & x > 2 \end{cases}
$$
